From: havok2-htwo <havok2-htwo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 12:53:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/40] docs: STRATA_MMQ_DIRECT (opt-in) and the STRATA_MMQ_TAB
 build option, with the measurements

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 docs/DETAILS.md | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/docs/DETAILS.md b/docs/DETAILS.md
index 49c49d1b..d59202f8 100644
--- a/docs/DETAILS.md
+++ b/docs/DETAILS.md
@@ -1704,6 +1704,25 @@ the pairs in which the switched arm won). They are here so you can try them on y
   `STRATA_ADAPT_NOWAIT` or `STRATA_DMA_BATCH`; otherwise the log says why it is off. RTX A4000, 6 interleaved pairs each,
   decode median tok/s: Q2_0 16 GB 62.5 -> 66.2 (+5.9%), Q2_0 as a 12 GB card 51.3 -> 54.2 (+5.7%), IQ3_XXS 16 GB 48.6 -> 52.6 (+8.3%),
   IQ3_XXS as 12 GB 39.3 -> 41.5 (+5.5%), 6/6 pairs faster every time.
+- **`STRATA_MMQ_DIRECT=1`: the prompt's MMQ reads resident experts in their cache slots** (CUDA, native packs). The prompt
+  path multiplies its experts in groups of 16 with llama.cpp's MMQ, which wants a group's weights in one buffer at a fixed
+  stride, so every expert a chunk routes to is copied there first (2.3-3.1 MB each, per chunk and layer), resident ones
+  too. With this on, a Strata-side copy of MMQ's outer scheduler (`src/prefill/moe_mmq_tab.cuh`) takes one pointer per
+  expert instead, and a resident expert is read where it is; streamed, peer-GPU and other experts are still copied. The
+  launch (tile size, stream-k split, fixup) is the one MMQ makes for the copied group and the tile code is llama.cpp's
+  own, so the answers are the same bits (`prefill_mmq_tab_test`: 874 products bitwise equal to the copied path, every type
+  and tile size). Each sized cache slot keeps 256 bytes after its expert, because MMQ reads up to 136 bytes past a
+  640-wide down matrix; they are zeroed before each group. That is about 2.3 MiB over 9,300 slots (one slot fewer out of
+  6,998 on one RTX 3090). `STRATA_MMQ_DIRECT=2` keeps that layout but copies as before (an A/B reference); 0 or unset is
+  the default path, byte for byte. Under HIP, or when CMake turned the build option `STRATA_MMQ_TAB` off (it does so, with
+  a message, when the llama.cpp tree's `mmq.cuh` differs from the one the copy mirrors), the variable is ignored.
+  Measured on 4x RTX 3090, UD-IQ4_XS, split `12,24,36` with `--trim-stage-weights` and `--batch 16` (every expert
+  resident), the engine driven directly, its "read in" time, medians of 8: a 170-token prompt 331 -> 251 ms (-24%) and
+  369 -> 290 ms (-21%) in two series, a 1,217-token prompt 773 -> 634 ms (-18%) and 838 -> 675 ms (-19%). Skipping
+  those copies altogether (wrong answers; the bound) gave 225 and 577 ms. On one RTX 3090 with 40 GiB of experts
+  streamed from RAM the copies of streamed experts stay, and the prompt time moved by 0-1.5%. Greedy answers with it on
+  were identical to off: `tools/batch_test16.py` (16 prompts, solo and batch), 20 prompts of 170 and 1,217 tokens, and
+  on one GPU (resident and streamed experts in the same groups), with `STRATA_PREFILL_CPU_SHARE=0` and no adaptive swaps.
 
 ## Thread placement: `--aux-cpus` (Linux, off by default)
 
